The post continued, &#x201C;Steven was a gifted and versatile performer, a superb graphic artist and a cherished member of our artistic family. His creativity and talent helped shape Interact in its earliest years, and his artistry, humor, warmth and generous spirit enriched everything he touched.&#x201D;

In addition to &#x201C;Cats,&#x201D; Hack had an extensive career as an actor with roles in &#x201C;American Horror Story,&#x201D; &#x201C;Ratched,&#x201D; &#x201C;Marvel&#x2019;s Agent Carter,&#x201D; "Days of Our Lives" and &#x201C;General Hospital.&#x201D; Other roles included "Amsterdam," "Angelyne," "Pam and Tommy" and "The West Wing."

He was born in St. Louis before attending college at the California Institute of the Arts and the American Academy of Dramatic Arts. He made his professional debut as one of the teenage dancers in the 1978 film &#x201C;Grease&#x201D; before heading to Broadway and accruing a long list of film and television titles.

The company concluded its post, &#x201C;As we remember Steven, we celebrate not only his many contributions to our company but also the joy of knowing and working with him. He will always hold a special place in Interact&#x2019;s history and in the hearts of those who knew him. We extend our heartfelt condolences to his family, friends and all who loved him. May his memory be a blessing.&#x201D;

As the nation remains enthralled by Nathan Fielder and Lance Oppenheim&#x2019;s Elizabeth Holmes film &#x201C;You Can See Everything,&#x201D; the first documentary to cross $100 million at the global box office in over two decades, arguably the most influential &#x2014; and the most prolific &#x2014; documentarian in American cinema&#x2019;s works have found a new home.

The complete works of Frederick Wiseman , the renowned documentary filmmaker behind &#x201C;Titicut Follies&#x201D; (1967), &#x201C;Hospital&#x201D; (1970) and &#x201C;Ex Libris: The New York Public Library&#x201D; (2017), have found a home with Janus Films . Wiseman produced, directed and edited 45 features that were released under Zipporah Films, Inc., his production and distribution company named for his wife, and they&#x2019;ll all be distributed by Janus Films in North America starting Jan. 1, 2027, joining an impressive library that includes films by greats like Agn&#xE8;s Varda, Ingmar Bergman, Akira Kurosawa and Chantal Akerman. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories 'NCIS: New York' Sells Across 135 Markets Globally (EXCLUSIVE) Tony Awards Will Remain on CBS Through 2032 Under New Five-Year Deal

Wiseman&#x2019;s early films, from &#x201C;Titicut Follies&#x201D; (1967) through &#x201C;State Legislature&#x201D; (2007), will be available in 4K (having been restored by Zipporah Films), while &#x201C;La Danse&#x201D; (2009) through &#x201C;Menus-Plaisirs &#x2014; Les Troisgros&#x201D; (2023) will be offered in their original digital formats. &#x201C;A director whose rigorously objective explorations of social institutions constitute one of the most revered bodies of work in American documentary filmmaking,&#x201D; according to The New York Times, Wiseman, who passed away earlier this year at the age of 96, crafted a one-of-a-kind filmography that chronicled the experiences of everyday people in the U.S. (and France). He was given the Honorary Award for lifetime achievement from the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences in 2016, and his works helped expose corruption in vulnerable communities. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;We couldn&#x2019;t imagine a better home in North America for Frederick Wiseman&#x2019;s films than with Janus Films,&#x201D; said Karen Konicek of Zipporah Films. &#x201C;It has been an honor to work alongside Fred for so many decades, producing and distributing his films. As Zipporah Films continues as the rights holder and distributor of the Wiseman Collection, we very much look forward to this new partnership with Janus Films and are excited to collaborate with them.&#x201D;

&#x201C;We are honored and thrilled to be the first distributor, in the U.S., outside of Zipporah Films, to be entrusted with licensing one of the greatest bodies of work in American cinema&#x201D; said Peter Becker, President of Janus Films and Criterion. &#x201C;We&#x2019;re looking forward to presenting these monumental works, theatrically, to new audiences across the country.&#x201D;

The full list of titles acquired: &#x201C;Titicut Follies&#x201D; (1967), &#x201C;High School&#x201D; (1968,) &#x201C;Law and Order&#x201D; (1969), &#x201C;Hospital&#x201D; (1970), &#x201C;Basic Training&#x201D; (1971), &#x201C;Essene&#x201D; (1972), &#x201C;Juvenile Court&#x201D; (1973), &#x201C;Primate&#x201D; (1974), &#x201C;Welfare&#x201D; (1975), &#x201C;Meat&#x201D; (1976), &#x201C;Canal Zone&#x201D; (1977), &#x201C;Sinai Field Mission&#x201D; (1978), &#x201C;Manoeuvre&#x201D; (1979), &#x201C;Model&#x201D; (1980), &#x201C;The Store&#x201D; (1983), &#x201C;Racetrack&#x201D; (1985), &#x201C;Blind&#x201D; (1986), &#x201C;Deaf&#x201D;(1986), &#x201C;Adjustment & Work&#x201D; (1986), &#x201C;Multi-handicapped&#x201D; (1986), &#x201C;Missile&#x201D; (1988), &#x201C;Near Death&#x201D; (1989), &#x201C;Central Park&#x201D; (1990), &#x201C;Aspen&#x201D; (1991), &#x201C;Zoo&#x201D; (1993), &#x201C;High School II&#x201D; (1994), &#x201C;Ballet&#x201D; (1995), &#x201C;La Com&#xE9;die-Fran&#xE7;aise ou L'amour jou&#xE9;&#x201D; (1996), &#x201C;Public Housing&#x201D; (1997), &#x201C;Belfast, Maine&#x201D; (1999), &#x201C;Domestic Violence&#x201D; (2001), &#x201C;Domestic Violence 2&#x201D; (2002), &#x201C;The Last Letter&#x201D; (2002), &#x201C;State Legislature&#x201D; (2007), &#x201C;La Danse: The Paris Opera Ballet&#x201D; (2009), &#x201C;Boxing Gym&#x201D; (2010), &#x201C;Crazy Horse&#x201D; (2011), &#x201C;At Berkeley&#x201D; (2013), &#x201C;National Gallery&#x201D; (2014), &#x201C;In Jackson Heights&#x201D; (2015), &#x201C;Ex Libris: The New York Public Library&#x201D; (2017), &#x201C;Monrovia, Indiana&#x201D; (2018), &#x201C;City Hall&#x201D; (2020), &#x201C;A Couple&#x201D; (2022) and &#x201C;Menus-Plaisirs, les Troisgros&#x201D; (2023).
